# NOTE: deliberately no blanket rule for hidden directories.
#
# Some terrain packages keep required data in dot-directories that Unity's own package
# export leaves out -- Digger's `.internal` data under Assets/DiggerData is the documented
# example. A rule like `.*/` would drop it silently, and the failure looks like "the cave
# scene opened but the caves are gone" on a fresh clone.
#
# Verify vendor data survives by opening a clean checkout, not by assuming that the scene
# file being present means its data is.

# Third-party Asset Store content.
#
# Licensed per seat, so it must not be redistributed through a shared repository -- this one
# has a public-facing remote. Each developer imports Gaia from their own account and the
# terrain textures arrive with it.
#
# Size is the lesser reason: these twelve files are about 186 MB against a few hundred KB of
# code, and git keeps whatever it is given forever.
/Assets/TerrainTextures/
/Assets/Procedural Worlds/

# Generated terrain (heights and splatmap) is our own output and small, so it stays tracked.
# Without the textures above, its layers will have dangling references and TerrainImport says
# so on load rather than silently rendering flat colour.
